## Worked Example

First discovery run: `Bus-Applications` (Linux userspace C++ dashboard on
Raspberry Pi — treated with the embedded bias: I2C/SPI/GPIO are the
architecture). Boundary `src`, scope `working-tree`.

Fundamental structure (tool output, pruned): App 1 --uses--> MPU6050 1
and ADS7830 1, both `--requires-->` I2CDevice (multiplicity **2**) `--uses-->`
`/dev/i2c-1` (shared, no arbiter). HC595 1 `--requires-->` SPIDevice `--uses-->`
`/dev/spidev0.0` (exclusive). LCD1602 `--uses-->` `/dev/gpiochip0` and a
bundled 6-line GPIO set (exclusive). Processor: raspberry-pi.

I/O: MPU6050 `init`/`read` → `MpuRead`; ADS7830 `readAnalog`; I2CDevice
register R/W; jobs capacity 1, caller-serial, no queue, sync none. App
sequential-poll, periods include 200 ms loop and 50 ms retry.

State (inferred): App Init → Poll → (io-error) Retry → Poll; single thread
so Poll and Retry do not overlap. LCD Unopened → LinesClaimed → Initialized.

Contention finding: two I2C clients, two `I2CDevice` instances, one bus,
no mutex — matches this repo's documented single-thread deviation.

**Invention overlay (same session, owner ask):** "proxies should resource-protect
`I2CDevice` so if App changes, each wrapper is truly for one chip." That is
invention, not more discovery. Overlay added `I2cDeviceGuard` (mult 2, mutex),
froze `(bus, addr)` (drop public `setAddress`), kept construction inside the
driver. A per-device guard is **ownership**; `I2cBusLock` would be a second
overlay only if MPU and ADS can be called concurrently. Artifact:
Bus-Applications `architecture-out/2026-09-09_working-tree-i2c/`
(`to_be.json`, `structure-i2c-to-be.mmd`).

Full generated discovery dump:
[worked-examples/bus-applications/](worked-examples/bus-applications/).

## Anti-Patterns / Failure Modes

- Dumping every struct (protocol helpers, flags) onto the first diagram.
- Drawing Mermaid from memory that disagrees with `model.json`.
- Counting `I2CDevice`/`SPIDevice` as a second peer on the bus they wrap.
- Treating a single-threaded poll as if it needed a mutex, or the reverse:
  hiding the shared bus because today's caller is serial.
- Sequence diagrams of the whole dashboard instead of a state machine of
  ownership.
- Mixing invented objects into a discovery diagram without labeling.
- Silently analyzing the whole tree when the PR/commit file set is empty.
- Skipping I/O/jobs because the structure chart "already looks complete."
- **Answering a follow-up only in chat while `session.md` stays stale.** The
  file is the record. Stamp `session refine` in the same turn. Chat is still
  where Mermaid *renders* — paste the fence there; don't substitute a path
  to a `.mmd` the owner cannot preview locally.
- **Telling the owner to open `architecture-out/*.mmd` in Cursor preview.**
  That preview often does not render Mermaid. The directory is gitignored, so
  a GitHub push of the app repo will not either.
- **Patching `extract.py` / `cli.py` imports mid-session** because
  `select_fundamental` was not found. Use `architect.py analyze` or
  `from architect import select_fundamental`. Log the miss; don't fork the tool.

## Quality Gate

Before `draft → validated`:

- [x] Deterministic tools exist (`scope`, `extract`, `diagram`, `analyze`,
      `diff`, `session`, `feedback`) and `self-test` passes.
- [x] One real discovery run on an owned embedded repo (Bus-Applications).
- [x] At least one invention overlay on a real owner question (`I2cDeviceGuard`
      / frozen addr on Bus-Applications I2C — overlay only, not C++).
- [x] Owner review of diagram crowding and object-template usefulness
      (agent-chat Mermaid render on SPI/I2C sessions: "great"; local Cursor
      preview of unpushed `.mmd` does not render — viewing surface is chat).
- [ ] Feedback log items from the first run either applied or wontfix'd.
- [ ] Owner sign-off (no auto-publish).

